==English==
{{was wotd|2008|March|21}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*h₂en-|*h₂welh₁-|id2=rule}}
{{back-form|en|ambivalence}}, from {{der|en|de|Ambivalenz}}, from {{der|en|la|ambi-||in two ways}} + {{m|la|valeō||be strong}}; equivalent to {{confix|en|ambi|valent}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/æmˈbɪv.ə.lənt/|a=RP,US}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-ambivalent.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-ambivalent.ogg|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|en-au-ambivalent.ogg|a=AU}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# [[simultaneously|Simultaneously]] experiencing or expressing [[oppose|opposing]] or [[contradictory]] [[feeling]]s, [[belief]]s, or [[motivation]]s.
# [[alternate|Alternately]] being or having one [[opinion]] or feeling, and then the [[opposite]].
#: {{ux|en|His feelings toward his parents are '''ambivalent'''.}}

====Usage notes====
''Ambivalent'' may be loosely used to denote lacking emotions rather than having conflicting emotions. More appropriate alternatives for a lack of emotion would be {{m|en|indifferent}} and {{m|en|apathetic}}.

====Synonyms====
* {{sense|simultaneously experiencing or expressing opposing feelings}} {{l|en|conflicted}}, {{l|en|uncertain}}, {{l|en|undecided}}, {{l|en|unresolved}}
* {{sense|alternately feeling opposing feelings}} {{l|en|vacillating}}, {{l|en|fluctuating}}, {{l|en|wavering}}

